1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,750
In April 1989, a woman out running in
Central Park at night was attacked,

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,240
beaten and left close to death.

3
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:12,240
Within about two days, police and
prosecutors had statements from

4
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:14,730
five teenagers. The
youngest were fourteen.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,610
Four of them described the
attack on videotape.

6
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:23,790
Each put himself at the scene, and put
the worst of it on the others.

7
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:29,430
The fifth, Yusef Salaam, who was
fifteen, never went on tape.

8
00:00:31,510 --> 00:00:34,910
Before he talked, a detective told him
his fingerprints could turn

9
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:35,900
up on her clothing.

10
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:37,520
There were no such prints.

11
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,260
At trial, the detective
acknowledged it had been a trick.

12
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:46,420
No physical evidence tied any
of the five to her.

13
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:50,080
The DNA found at the scene
matched none of them.

14
00:00:50,590 --> 00:00:55,250
They were convicted anyway, in 1990, and
spent from about six to about

15
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:56,760
thirteen years locked up.

16
00:00:58,740 --> 00:01:03,140
In 2002, a man serving life for other
crimes said he had attacked

17
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,170
her, alone. His DNA matched.

18
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:09,920
The Manhattan district attorney asked
the court to throw out every

19
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:11,630
conviction, and it did.

20
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,270
Today the five men are known as
the Exonerated Five.

21
00:01:17,370 --> 00:01:20,970
And what that detective did is
something police in every American

22
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:23,060
state may still do to adults.

23
00:01:24,030 --> 00:01:25,800
Lying about the evidence is allowed.

24
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:30,180
Since 2021, ten states have
restricted it for children.

25
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:35,570
So, should police be allowed to lie
to the people they question?

26
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:39,770
A bluff about the evidence can get a
guilty person to tell the truth.

27
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:43,640
It can also convince an innocent one
that confessing is the only way

28
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:47,490
out. And does the answer change when the
person in the room is fifteen?
